# Yanlai Workshop

Yanlai Workshop is a SaaS product by Yanlai for small independent automotive workshops.

We are currently building an MVP. Prioritize a functional, simple and maintainable product over unnecessary complexity.

## Product structure

Yanlai Workshop has three main interfaces that share the same underlying data:

1. Reception — primarily used on a tablet.
   Purpose: receive a vehicle quickly and create a Repair Order.

2. Payments / POS — primarily used on a tablet.
   Purpose: quickly charge services or completed Repair Orders and eventually print tickets.

3. Management — primarily used on desktop/web.
   Purpose: manage workshop operations, including Repair Orders, diagnosis, budgets/estimates, repairs, customers, vehicles and later invoicing and stock.

## Core entity

The Repair Order (OR / Orden de Reparación) is the central operational entity.

Main intended workflow:

Vehicle arrives
→ Reception
→ Repair Order created
→ Diagnosis
→ Budget / Estimate
→ Repair
→ Payment
→ Ticket or Invoice
→ Repair Order closed

Do not break this workflow without explicit instruction.

## UX principles

- Reduce clicks and unnecessary steps.
- Interfaces must be clear for workshop employees who are not technical users.
- Reception and Payments are task-focused tablet interfaces, not reduced copies of the management interface.
- Management can contain more detailed information and administrative actions.
- Preserve the existing Yanlai Workshop visual language unless explicitly asked to redesign something.
- Do not make unrelated visual changes when implementing functionality.

## Development rules

- Work incrementally. Prefer small, controlled changes.
- Do not rewrite large parts of the application unless explicitly requested.
- Do not introduce new frameworks, libraries or architectural patterns unless they are genuinely necessary.
- Before changing existing architecture, inspect and understand the current implementation.
- Reuse existing components, styles, data structures and patterns when appropriate.
- Do not modify unrelated files or features.
- Do not delete existing functionality unless explicitly instructed.
- Keep the MVP scope in mind and avoid overengineering.
- When requirements are ambiguous and the decision could materially affect product behavior or architecture, ask before implementing.
- Fix the root cause of errors rather than hiding them.
- Keep code readable and maintainable.

## Validation

After implementing a requested change:

- Check for obvious errors.
- Run the relevant available validation/build commands when appropriate.
- Explain briefly what was changed.
- State which files were modified.
- Mention any important limitation or unresolved issue.
- Never claim something works if it has not actually been verified.

## Collaboration

The product owner is not a programmer.

When explaining technical matters:
- use clear language;
- avoid unnecessary jargon;
- explain what the result means for the product;
- do not require the product owner to manually edit code unless specifically requested.
